[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two harness details that matter for correctness of the *test*, not the library:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * The Rust `cdylib` is opened with `RTLD_DEEPBIND`. The C libraries are in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   global scope and define the same names (`SPX_thash`, `DRBG_ctx`, …) with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   default visibility, so without it the Rust library's own GOT/PLT entries get
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   interposed by the C definitions and the comparison silently degenerates to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   C-against-C. The harness asserts that the two resolved addresses differ.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `cargo test` does not reliably rebuild a `crate-type = ["cdylib"]` artifact, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the runner always does an explicit `cargo build --release` first, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   harness cross-checks `crypto_sign_bytes()` from both `.so`s against its own
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   derivation to catch a stale library.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
