[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong, and what I fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne real translation defect[22m (Phase A/D symbol parity): [38;5;10mcst[0m was missing from the Rust [38;5;10m.so[0m. [38;5;10mlib/blake/src/blake512.c[0m declares its round constants as [38;5;10mconst u64 cst[16][0m [3mwithout[23m [38;5;10mstatic[0m (unlike the file-local copy in [38;5;10mblake256.c[0m), so the C library exports it as a read-only data symbol. The Rust side had it as a private [38;5;10mconst CST[0m. Fixed by adding an exported [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub static cst: [u64; 16][0m in [38;5;10msrc/backend/blake/blake512.rs[0m. That was the only gap — no module had been skipped, so no new translation was required and no stubs were introduced.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mTwo harness defects I had to fix before the tests meant anything.[22m Both would have produced false passes:[0m[0m
- The C libraries are loaded [38;5;10mRTLD_GLOBAL[0m (the core has undefined references to the backend), and they define the [3msame[23m names as the Rust [38;5;10mcdylib[0m. Without [38;5;10mRTLD_DEEPBIND[0m on the Rust library, the Rust library's own GOT/PLT entries were interposed by the C definitions — [38;5;10mrandombytes_init[0m mutated the C [38;5;10mDRBG_ctx[0m, and the comparison degenerated to C-against-C. The harness now asserts the two resolved addresses differ.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcargo test[0m does not reliably rebuild a [38;5;10mcrate-type = ["cdylib"][0m artifact, so a leftover [38;5;10m.so[0m from another feature combination gets dlopened. The harness now cross-checks [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_bytes()[0m from both [38;5;10m.so[0ms against its own independent parameter derivation.[0m[0m
