[38;5;252m[1m## Two C behaviours worth knowing about (reproduced, not "fixed")[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mhash_blake.c[0m calls [38;5;10mblakeX_update(&S, m, mlen)[0m while [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m/[38;5;10mblake512_update[0m take their length in [1mbits[22m and [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m only [38;5;10mdatalen >> 3[0m bytes. With the blake backend, a message therefore influences its signature only through the first [38;5;10mmlen/8[0m bytes, and only when [38;5;10mmlen % 8 == 0[0m — otherwise [38;5;10mblake*_final[0m never reaches a compression at all. Measured on blake-128f: mlen 64 → 8 sensitive bytes, 128 → 16, but 70 and 100 → 0. The Rust matches exactly; [38;5;10mERRORS.md[0m row E2 records it. The other three backends absorb every byte.[0m[0m
